{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-559-561", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-02-damascus-jewish-community", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus, War 2.20 (66 CE): the people of Damascus slaughtered 10,000 Jews living among them 'in one hour's time' - a large, settled Jewish community in Damascus in the same period; it attests the community, not the synagogues as buildings."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-559-561", "type": "attests", "to": "place:damascus", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, War 2.20 names Damascus and its Jewish population in the 1st century CE."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-559-561", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-19-22-preached-damascus", "grade": "C", "note": "The same passage confirms the Jewish population of Damascus among whom Acts says Saul proclaimed Jesus; it attests the audience, not the preaching."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-566-567", "type": "attests", "to": "place:lydda", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, War 2.20 names Lydda (in Manasseh's command portion in 66 CE)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-2-566-567", "type": "attests", "to": "place:joppa", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, War 2.20 names Joppa (in Manasseh's command portion in 66 CE)."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-16-160-165", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-01-02-official-letters-custom", "grade": "C", "note": "Ant. 16.6: the Jews of Asia and Cyrene sent ambassadors, and Caesar and Agrippa 'sent letters to the same purpose to the governors of the provinces' on behalf of diaspora Jewish communities and their synagogues - the letter system between a central authority and diaspora communities; it attests the practice, not any high priest's letters or this arrest warrant."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-15-331-caesarea", "type": "attests", "to": "place:caesarea", "grade": "A", "note": "Josephus, Ant. 15.8: Strato's Tower 'was by him named Cesarea' - Herod's new port city, the same place Acts 9:30 names; built c. 22-10 BCE, before the verse."}
{"from": "text:strabo-14-5-13", "type": "attests", "to": "place:tarsus", "grade": "A", "note": "Strabo, Geography 14.5.13 names Tarsus, 'extremely powerful' and 'the character of being the capital' (of Cilicia), with famous schools of philosophy - the city Acts 9:11 and 9:30 name."}
{"from": "site:street-called-straight", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-11-straight-street", "grade": "B", "note": "The Street Called Straight in Damascus is the old decumanus maximus, the main east-west Roman road, built in the Seleucid period and still running through the old city - the specific street setting Acts 9:11 presupposes; it attests the street, not the house of Judah."}
{"from": "text:mishnah-bava-batra-2-9", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-43-tanning-custom", "grade": "B", "note": "Mishnah Bava Batra 2:9: a tannery must be distanced fifty cubits from the city because of its odors - tanning as a noxious trade kept outside the residential area; redacted c. 200 CE and general, it may not describe 30s-40s Joppa exactly, and it does not name Simon's tannery. Regraded C to B per the architect's Mishnah ruling (2026-11-15): the Mishnah names the same practice the claim states."}
{"from": "artifact:eshmunazar-sarcophagus", "type": "attests", "to": "place:sharon-plain", "grade": "D", "note": "The Phoenician inscription (KAI 14) grants the Sidonian king 'Dor and Joppa, the mighty lands of Dagon, which are in the plain of Sharon' - an ancient source naming the Sharon plain, but a 5th-century BCE royal gift list, not the 1st-century district, and the sarcophagus reached the Louvre through an 1855 commercial discovery without controlled excavation, so its authenticity cannot be secured (the recorded find spot is Magharet Abloun, Sidon)."}
{"from": "artifact:eshmunazar-sarcophagus", "type": "attests", "to": "place:joppa", "grade": "D", "note": "The same inscription names 'Dor and Joppa' - an ancient naming of Joppa, but 5th-century BCE Phoenician context, and a commercially discovered sarcophagus (grade D per the unprovenanced rule); Josephus (War 2.20) is the stronger A-grade naming."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-18-113-115", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-23-25-nabataean-damascus", "grade": "C", "note": "Ant. 18.5: in c. 36 CE Aretas IV was the reigning Nabataean king whose army destroyed Antipas's forces near Perea - his kingship and military reach in the period, but the passage says nothing about Damascus; it does not attest Nabataean control of the city."}
{"from": "artifact:judean-desert-textiles", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:ac-09-39-dorcas-garments", "grade": "C", "note": "Woollen textiles and clothing from the Cave of Letters (c. 135 CE) attest the class of household-woven coats and garments in Judean households a century later; they say nothing of Tabitha or Joppa."}
{"from": "text:galatians-1-17", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-09-19-22-preached-damascus", "note": "Paul's own letter, Galatians 1:17: 'nor did I go up to Jerusalem to those who were apostles before me, but I went away into Arabia. Then I returned to Damascus.' Galatians places Arabia between the revelation and the return to Damascus; Acts 9 never mentions Arabia. Biblical cross-reference, not evidence."}
{"from": "text:2-corinthians-11-32-33", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-09-23-25-escape-basket", "note": "Paul's own account (2 Corinthians 11:32-33): the governor (ethnarch) under King Aretas guarded the Damascenes' city and Paul was let down in a basket through a window by the wall. Acts 9 says the Jews watched the gates and the disciples lowered him through the wall in a basket. Same escape, different detail; biblical, not independent evidence."}
{"from": "text:2-corinthians-11-32-33", "type": "related_to", "to": "claim:ac-09-23-25-nabataean-damascus", "note": "2 Corinthians 11:32 is the only direct statement that Aretas's ethnarch exercised authority at Damascus; as a biblical text it cannot attest the claim it grounds - the dispute is recorded in the claim's note."}
{"from": "place:tarsus", "type": "related_to", "to": "person:paul-of-tarsus", "note": "Tarsus is named as Saul's home city (Acts 9:11, 9:30; also Acts 21:39; 22:3); a place never attests a person, so this is a link, not evidence."}
